{
  "id": "house-robber-ii",
  "questionId": "house-robber-ii",
  "slug": "house-robber-ii",
  "title": "House Robber II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Dp 1d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "the only thing making this harder than plain House Robber is the wraparound edge between house 0 and the last house",
    "any valid selection either excludes house 0 or excludes the last house (it can never rob both, since they're adjacent) - can you use that to split into two simpler cases?",
    "run the ordinary House Robber recurrence once on the houses excluding the last one, and once on the houses excluding the first one",
    "the answer is the max of those two linear runs - watch out for the n=1 case, where there's no wraparound conflict at all"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "applying plain House Robber directly and missing the wraparound adjacency between the first and last house",
    "excluding both endpoints instead of running two separate linear passes that each exclude just one",
    "forgetting the n=1 special case, where slicing off 'the last house' from a single-house list produces an empty array"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Same setup as robbing houses along a street, except this time the houses\nare arranged in a circle: house 0 and the last house are also considered\nadjacent. `nums[i]` is the cash in house `i`, and you cannot rob two\nadjacent houses, including the wraparound pair.\n\n**Return the maximum total you can rob.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 100",
      "0 <= nums[i] <= 1000"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single house (rob it, no wraparound conflict possible)",
      "two houses (they're adjacent both ways, so rob the larger one)",
      "three houses in a circle, where any two are adjacent to each other"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [2, 3, 2]",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "house 0 and house 2 are adjacent through the wraparound, so you can only take one house - the best is house 1"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 1]",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "rob houses 1 and 3 (not adjacent, since it's houses 0 and 3 that wrap around): 2 + ... actually rob houses 0 and 2: 1 + 3 = 4"
      }
    ],
